00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.309 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to welcome back to the  program Larry Norden who's deputy  director of the democracy program at NYU  Law School's Brennan Center for Justice  you know Larry for all the talk about  what took place during the last election  and sort of the importance of these  midterm elections depending on what your  political views are there's surprisingly  little discussion of what has been done  to ensure election integrity in these  midterms that are coming up in November  so I mean I guess first big picture it  for us  rate your confidence that we're going to  have full and fair elections in November  of 2018 mm-hmm well I think a good place  to start when we're talking about this  is what we mean by election interference  that that term gets used a lot  particularly as applies to the 2016  election and it can mean a number of  things so in 2016 we know that the  Russians Kremlin directed purchased  political planets on sorry that  purchased political ads on platforms  like Facebook and Google and Twitter and  elsewhere and that's something that  we've gotten somewhat of a handle on the  government hasn't necessarily done all  that much  I expect that we're likely to see some  more of that stuff but that the social  media companies themselves have done a  little bit more about that so that at  least if it and when that happens again  in the 2016 election and every reason to  believe that will as I said it'll be  it'll be a little bit more obvious and  advocacy groups the public reporters can  call that out when it happens so that's  one aspect I think when you talk about  election integrity a lot of people talk  about election integrity they mean a  second aspect which is the election  infrastructure itself and there again  there are there are a few parts to that  there there are the voting machines  which i think is what most people go to  immediately  when they think about is somebody  messing with our elections through  cyberattacks and making sure that those  are recording votes accurately but there  are also many other election systems  that we use to conduct elections there  the registration systems right that's  what tells us who is entitled and not  not entitled to go vote on those  machines in the first place there are  electronic poll books that might be used  in polling places that we use to to  provide that registration data in the  polling place on Election Day of their  election websites which might tell  people what polling places to go to and  also provide election night reporting to  vote totals and we need to protect all  of those so in terms of how confident I  am I we're doing much more than we ever  had to secure that election  infrastructure in part in response to  the 2016 election there are far more  cyber trainings of election officials to  make them aware of how to defend the  systems Congress recently allocated 380  million dollars to the states to spend  on election system security and and  there have been upgrades that have been  happening at the state and county level  of these various systems having said all  that we're not doing as much as I would  like and certainly you can I can point  to specific things ahead of the 2018  election that I'd like to see happen  that probably aren't going to happen and  and and I think we really need to push  for ahead of 2020 most obviously we  still have paperless voting machines in  the US which election system security  experts are nearly unanimous the  Department of Homeland Security in the  intelligence agencies the intelligence  committees from the Senate and the house  have said these need to be replaced with  systems that produce a paper record so  that if there is some kind of intrusion  against these systems we can detect it  and we have a backup record that we can  use to to make sure that votes are  accurate so a lot of those along those  lines if I if I can  I've been here with a few school  questions when we talked about the  reports that although there's no  evidence that vote totals were changed  by any kind of Russian targeting of  voting systems that those systems in 21  states at least 21 states were targeted  it appears as though in it in at least  one case Russian agents were able to  access those systems and exactly what  that means I guess is something we could  discuss when you talk about having a  paper verification of what votes were  cast is that a mechanism that would help  to counteract whatever it is that might  be done through the intrusions that were  attempted in 21 States or are we talking  about two different things there yeah I  want to be clear about this because this  gets really confusing especially the  people that don't spend a lot of time in  elections or studying elections from  everything we know that's been reported  about those 21 states those were not  attacks on voting machines those were  not attempts to break into voting  machines they were attempts there were  either attempts to scan or to breach  either registration systems or election  websites right that's not their votes  are counted and totaled the one breach  that we know about and actually there  was a Senate Intelligence Committee  report that indicated that it was more  than one state that had a breach they  didn't say how many they said a small  number but one state that we do know  about is Illinois and that was there a  breach in to their registration system  and there was apparently an attempt to  change some information in the  registration rules that was unsuccessful  now you can imagine if somebody was  successful in changing the roles the  registration roles that that would be  that could cause a lot of problems if  you know names are removed from a list  or address exchange so that when they  show up to the polling place they're not  listed in the poll books that that could  create problems a person it might create  long lines the person should be given a  provisional ballot but unless they  figure out what happened that ballot  might not be counted so it's really  important that states have have backups  of all of the registry  information and that they're auditing it  that they're checking it and if there  are problems that there's some way to go  back and figure out who should have been  able to vote and to count those votes  now that's completely separate from the  other issue that that we were just  talking about which is the voting  machines themselves now those you know  it is theoretically possible that you  could have malicious software reach  those machines it's theoretically  possible that those machines could  report incorrect results because they're  running on software and that's where  having a paper record now there's no  evidence that that happened I want to be  clear in the 2016 election right but  that's something that we want to be  really concerned about and we want to  make sure that we have something in  place that will allow us to detect if  that happened and allow us to recover if  that happened and the best something in  place that we could have our paper  records that the voter fills out or  reviews that we know the voter said this  is my vote since you work on on this  issue from a few angles Larry I'm  curious and if this starts to get beyond  sort of your focus by up by all means  tell us that that you're sort of  speculating but if you were another  country and your goal was to alter what  would be the natural result of an  election in the United States what would  actually be the most pragmatic path to  that would it be trying to actually  change vote tallies in machines to the  extent that it's theoretically possible  would it be disinformation campaigns  through social media to actually get  people to vote in a different way what  what like I guess what I'm trying to say  is if we want to think about how we're  most vulnerable to the election results  being altered what are the actual most  effective paths to that if you're a  foreign entity you know one thing one  thing I want to be clear about actually  I think the good starting point is to  look at what the Russian specifically  have done in other countries because  they have interfered far more directly  in other countries elections in Eastern  Europe than they  than they apparently did in the United  States in 2016 and one of the things  that the Intelligence Agency's  intelligence community has told us and  the Senate Intelligence Committee said  this as well  primary objective of the Russians  wherever they've operated is just  undermine confidence in the system in  general right in 2016 they actually did  have an additional goal of of favoring  one candidate in the presidential race  over another but but their their  overarching goal always is just to sow  distrust in the system and and what the  Russians have done elsewhere pretty  clearly is they have targeted election  night reporting websites that deal with  reporting either turnout or vote totals  that wouldn't necessarily at the end of  the day change the outcome of the  election if you have the kinds of  redundancies that we have in place in  the United States but they would do an  awful sorry about my light there no  problem we can see it could have a big  impact on casting doubt in the election  outcomes and if you combine that with  social media and again if you look at  what the the Facebook ads that were just  released that the Russians had paid for  in the 2016 election some of them were  focused on how you just can't trust the  system there's no point in voting so  there's this this just general flow of  information that is meant to cast doubt  on the entire objective of liberal  democracy so that's point one now going  to if I actually wanted to go in and  change the results of have some impact  on changing the results of an election  look there's election night reporting we  tally all our votes from teens generally  in a central place in a county of a  state and so I might target those but  again there  didn't cease so you have you know a  County might be reporting its votes that  come in from all these polling places  but then you have the individual  appalling places and machines that are  reporting results so there's a good  chance that you might that you would if  you have the procedures in place catch  if there was at the county level of a  state level with the tallies what we  call tally servers if somehow those  numbers worker were incorrect that you  would find them and of course again  going back to what I said earlier that  might be if you're if you're Russia and  you're trying to undermine confidence in  the system that might be good enough and  you know if I wanted to go after the  voting machines where votes are counted  the most obvious place to go would be  where there were no paper records so you  wouldn't have this independent record to  check and and you know this doesn't take  a specialist in elections to understand  you know you took you would target  critical counties and battleground  states if we're doing something like a  presidential election and hope for the  best in the limited time we have left  other than the paper records of the  ballots that are cast what would be the  other one or two things that you'd like  changed well one thing for sure and and  this is something that the Senate  Intelligence Committee in its report  emphasized is is doing thorough  vulnerability assessments this is  something that the Department of  Homeland Security offers it looks at the  entire system and basically says here is  where your weaknesses are you need to  understand where your weaknesses are and  fix them and that could be in the  registration system it could be an  election websites it could be in you  know in the voting machines themselves  in the way that you're totaling results  and reporting them understanding all of  that is and where the weaknesses are is  really important and only 14 states last  I checked had undertaken this kind of  vulnerability assessment through DHS the  really detailed one so it that's number  one I'd like to see more states doing  that secondly is just upgrading  infrastructure more generally voting  machines are really old in the United  States we've got 43 states that are  using machines that are no longer  manufactured  which too often means election officials  of buying spare parts on eBay which I  don't think is particularly safe you  could say the same thing about  registration databases forty states are  using databases that were created well  over a decade ago they may be running on  meticulous oft where if it's no longer  vendor supported which means it's not  getting the kind of security patches  that it should so just a general  investment in upgrading those things  three hundred eighty million dollars  that Congress just gave to the states as  a start for that kind of upgrade but we  definitely need more money than that  Larry Norton deputy director of the  democracy program at NYU Law School's  Brennan Center for Justice Larry thanks  for talking to us about this again  thanks as always thanks for having me on

